WEBVTT

1
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.001
Let's start with app dot js. This is where
Express connects the main parts

2
00:00:48.036 --> 00:00:52.900
of the application. Requests that start with
slash api go to the API routes.

3
00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:56.732
The travel pages have their own route, and the
public folder holds things

4
00:00:56.778 --> 00:01:01.179
like images and styles. Handlebars builds the
pages that customers see.

5
00:01:01.504 --> 00:01:04.255
The Angular admin app talks to the same
backend.

6
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:06.484
So there is already some separation here.

7
00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:09.596
I don't need to start over or replace the
whole design.

8
00:01:09.874 --> 00:01:12.417
This file also handles a few common errors.

9
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.609
If a request contains broken JSON, the server
sends back a four hundred

10
00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:21.125
response. If something unexpected goes wrong,
it sends a general server

11
00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:24.701
error instead of showing the full error to the
person using the site.

12
00:01:25.026 --> 00:01:28.219
Those are useful protections, and I want to
keep them when I make changes.

13
00:01:28.544 --> 00:01:30.750
The part I'd clean up is the trip controller.

14
00:01:30.994 --> 00:01:35.719
Right now, it checks requests, applies trip
rules, and talks to the database.

15
00:01:36.044 --> 00:01:38.296
I'd split those jobs into smaller pieces.

16
00:01:38.366 --> 00:01:40.850
The controller would handle the request and
response.

17
00:01:40.943 --> 00:01:42.777
A service would handle the trip rules.

18
00:01:42.928 --> 00:01:45.459
A separate file would handle database calls.

19
00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:49.267
That way, changing a trip rule wouldn't mean
digging through code for all

20
00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:52.599
three jobs. It should also make the tests
easier to follow.

21
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.864
Here is the save function in the Angular form.

22
00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.231
Before it sends anything, it checks whether
the form is valid and whether

23
00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:01.377
a save is already running. That second check
helps prevent someone from

24
00:02:01.447 --> 00:02:04.431
sending the same request twice by clicking
Save again.

25
00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:08.250
If the form needs fixing, it marks the fields
so the person can see the

26
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.476
errors. Otherwise, it either updates an
existing trip or adds a new one.

27
00:02:12.918 --> 00:02:17.050
After a successful save, it returns to the
list and shows a message.

28
00:02:17.294 --> 00:02:20.046
One detail I want to keep is the original trip
code.

29
00:02:20.162 --> 00:02:23.866
Someone can edit the code in the form, so the
app needs both values.

30
00:02:23.935 --> 00:02:26.675
The old code tells the server which trip to
find.

31
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:31.017
The new code is part of the update. For
example, if I change a code from

32
00:02:31.098 --> 00:02:34.326
A to B, the server still has to find A first.

33
00:02:34.651 --> 00:02:38.134
The data service sends the request with the
login token, and the backend

34
00:02:38.215 --> 00:02:42.348
passes it to the trip controller. The form
checks are helpful, but someone

35
00:02:42.383 --> 00:02:45.158
can send a request without using this form at
all.

36
00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:50.614
The server needs its own checks. I'd test
missing fields, bad values, duplicate

37
00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:53.981
codes, and attempts to update a trip that
doesn't exist.

38
00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:56.617
I'd also test changing the code itself.

39
00:02:56.895 --> 00:03:00.309
The goal is to keep the current save flow
while making sure those less

40
00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:02.538
common cases behave properly.

41
00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.946
This update function shows why I'd split up
the trip controller.

42
00:03:06.004 --> 00:03:09.649
It checks the trip code, picks fields from the
request, checks for missing

43
00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:14.037
values, updates the database, and decides
which response to send back.

44
00:03:14.188 --> 00:03:18.612
It also handles database errors. None of that
is unusual for a small app,

45
00:03:18.705 --> 00:03:21.421
but it makes this one function responsible for
a lot.

46
00:03:21.746 --> 00:03:25.682
As I add more trip rules, it will get harder
to change one thing without

47
00:03:25.717 --> 00:03:29.908
affecting something else. There are details
here that already work well.

48
00:03:30.187 --> 00:03:34.854
The update asks Mongoose to return the changed
trip, and it turns on validation

49
00:03:34.912 --> 00:03:37.501
for the update. I'd keep both settings.

50
00:03:37.826 --> 00:03:41.808
In the new layout, the controller would pass
the request to the trip service.

51
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:45.628
The service would check the trip rules and ask
the database code to save

52
00:03:45.663 --> 00:03:49.831
the result. If a trip code is already taken,
the controller would still

53
00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:54.591
send a clear conflict response. The person
using the app shouldn't lose

54
00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.667
useful feedback just because I've moved some
code.

55
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:02.648
I also don't want to overdo this. More files
aren't automatically better.

56
00:04:02.973 --> 00:04:06.828
I'd start with the trip functions I'm actually
changing and keep the new

57
00:04:06.886 --> 00:04:11.681
pieces small. The current code already lets
tests supply a fake model,

58
00:04:11.797 --> 00:04:15.535
which is useful. I'd keep those tests running
as I move things around,

59
00:04:15.651 --> 00:04:18.728
so I can tell whether I've changed behavior by
mistake.

60
00:04:18.734 --> 00:04:23.006
The trip routes already require a login token
for adding, editing, or deleting

61
00:04:23.065 --> 00:04:27.720
a trip. Reading trips is public. That makes
sense for a travel site: anyone

62
00:04:27.743 --> 00:04:31.191
should be able to browse, but changing the
catalog should be restricted.

63
00:04:31.702 --> 00:04:34.430
The problem is that registration is also
public.

64
00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:39.121
A new account gets a token, and the token
check lets that request continue.

65
00:04:39.724 --> 00:04:43.359
I don't see another check here that asks
whether the account is actually

66
00:04:43.417 --> 00:04:47.724
an administrator. So the missing question is,
who gets permission to manage

67
00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:51.102
trips? Being logged in doesn't answer that by
itself.

68
00:04:51.311 --> 00:04:56.640
I'd either restrict how admin accounts are
created or add an admin role

69
00:04:56.698 --> 00:04:59.694
that the server checks before allowing
changes.

70
00:05:00.135 --> 00:05:02.504
I'd settle that rule before writing the code.

71
00:05:02.887 --> 00:05:07.020
This finding comes from following the routes
and login checks in the source.

72
00:05:07.624 --> 00:05:11.130
I haven't tried it against a live website, so
I'm not claiming that I've

73
00:05:11.165 --> 00:05:16.064
tested an attack on a deployed app. To verify
the fix, I'd test three

74
00:05:16.122 --> 00:05:19.234
cases. A person with no token should be turned
away.

75
00:05:19.559 --> 00:05:23.332
A logged-in person without admin permission
should also be turned away.

76
00:05:23.576 --> 00:05:26.455
An approved admin should be able to reach the
trip function.

77
00:05:26.838 --> 00:05:29.497
Those tests need to use the real permission
checks.

78
00:05:29.776 --> 00:05:33.142
Hiding the Edit button in Angular would
improve the interface, but it wouldn't

79
00:05:33.189 --> 00:05:35.731
stop someone from sending the request
directly.

80
00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.985
The current backend test run passed all
twenty-one tests.

81
00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:43.594
They cover things like login, registration,
trip responses, token checks,

82
00:05:43.617 --> 00:05:46.682
and password hashing. That's a useful starting
point.

83
00:05:46.891 --> 00:05:49.956
It means I have checks I can run again while
changing the code.

84
00:05:50.281 --> 00:05:53.242
But I need to be clear about what those tests
actually do.

85
00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:56.795
Passing them doesn't mean I've tested every
part of the app together.

86
00:05:57.038 --> 00:06:01.090
For example, the route test on screen uses a
test function that lets every

87
00:06:01.172 --> 00:06:04.701
request through. That's fine for checking
whether a route calls the right

88
00:06:04.736 --> 00:06:07.952
handler. It doesn't tell me whether the real
route blocks someone without

89
00:06:07.998 --> 00:06:12.027
permission. The controller tests also use fake
database objects.

90
00:06:12.236 --> 00:06:16.984
The duplicate-code test supplies a database-
style error; it doesn't actually

91
00:06:17.031 --> 00:06:19.817
create two records with the same code in
MongoDB.

92
00:06:20.258 --> 00:06:23.986
And checking that the update includes
runValidators doesn't prove that

93
00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:26.575
every field rule works in a real database.

94
00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.172
I'd keep these fast tests and add a few tests
that connect the real pieces.

95
00:06:31.555 --> 00:06:35.282
Those would use a separate test database and
cover permissions, bad trip

96
00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.324
data, duplicate codes, and database failures.

97
00:06:38.766 --> 00:06:42.191
I'd also check the Angular form's loading and
error states.

98
00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.186
I haven't run the live database or browser
tests yet.

99
00:06:45.465 --> 00:06:49.331
When I report results later, I'll separate
what was checked with fake objects

100
00:06:49.482 --> 00:06:52.314
from what was checked with the actual app and
database.

101
00:06:52.334 --> 00:06:54.064
The current list function is pretty simple.

102
00:06:54.238 --> 00:06:58.069
It asks the database for all trips, then sends
the whole list back.

103
00:06:58.394 --> 00:07:02.504
It doesn't filter by budget, compare trip
lengths, or rank anything.

104
00:07:02.783 --> 00:07:04.547
That matters for the enhancement plan.

105
00:07:04.582 --> 00:07:08.379
I'm adding a way to rank trips; I'm not
replacing a ranking system that's

106
00:07:08.413 --> 00:07:13.069
already here. This function shows where the
trip data currently enters

107
00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.585
that part of the app. Sending every trip is
manageable with a small catalog.

108
00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:21.892
With a much larger catalog, there's more data
to load, hold in memory,

109
00:07:22.008 --> 00:07:26.606
and send over the network. I can't tell
exactly how long the database query

110
00:07:26.722 --> 00:07:29.218
will take just by reading this function.

111
00:07:29.346 --> 00:07:33.212
I'd need to measure it. But I can see that the
response grows as the catalog

112
00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.462
grows. That gives me a reason to think about
filtering and how many results

113
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.384
the app really needs at once. There is another
behavior I'd review.

114
00:07:42.709 --> 00:07:45.890
An empty list currently returns a not-found
response.

115
00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:48.549
The customer side treats that as an error.

116
00:07:48.827 --> 00:07:53.518
I'd rather have a normal empty list with a
message like, no trips are available

117
00:07:53.553 --> 00:07:58.835
right now. A request for one specific trip
that doesn't exist could still

118
00:07:58.905 --> 00:08:02.202
return not found. I'd update the tests and the
client together if I make

119
00:08:02.237 --> 00:08:05.523
that change, so they agree on what an empty
result means.

120
00:08:05.534 --> 00:08:09.412
Before I rank trips, I need to fix how some of
the values are stored.

121
00:08:09.656 --> 00:08:13.649
Price and trip length are both strings in the
Angular type and in the database

122
00:08:13.696 --> 00:08:16.355
model. That works for showing text on a page.

123
00:08:16.564 --> 00:08:18.932
It gets awkward when I want to compare
numbers.

124
00:08:19.141 --> 00:08:23.530
A label like three nights slash four days is
easy for a person to read,

125
00:08:23.808 --> 00:08:27.384
but the ranking code shouldn't have to guess
which number to use.

126
00:08:27.709 --> 00:08:31.076
I'd store the price as a whole number of cents
and the trip length as a

127
00:08:31.146 --> 00:08:35.081
whole number of days. For example, a price of
one hundred fifty dollars

128
00:08:35.128 --> 00:08:38.030
and fifty cents would be fifteen thousand
fifty cents.

129
00:08:38.413 --> 00:08:41.490
The page can still format that as a normal
price.

130
00:08:41.815 --> 00:08:46.273
The same idea applies to trip length: store a
clear number for calculations,

131
00:08:46.517 --> 00:08:50.418
then display a friendly label. Changing the
TypeScript type alone won't

132
00:08:50.476 --> 00:08:54.540
fix the records already in MongoDB. Those
records need to be checked and

133
00:08:54.586 --> 00:08:59.021
converted too. I'd reject values that don't
make sense instead of quietly

134
00:08:59.068 --> 00:09:03.816
treating them as zero. I'd also decide how
rounding works and what the

135
00:09:03.897 --> 00:09:07.891
app should do with a zero budget. That's
important if the score divides

136
00:09:07.938 --> 00:09:11.526
by the budget. Getting these rules settled
first keeps the ranking code

137
00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:15.577
simpler. It can compare clean numbers instead
of repeatedly trying to read

138
00:09:15.635 --> 00:09:17.656
numbers out of display text.

139
00:09:17.667 --> 00:09:21.719
The plan is to rank trips using a person's
budget and preferred trip length.

140
00:09:22.102 --> 00:09:25.817
Before choosing an algorithm, I need to decide
what a good match means.

141
00:09:26.096 --> 00:09:29.439
My starting idea is to leave out trips that
cost more than the budget.

142
00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.071
Then, for the trips that remain, I'd give a
better score to trips that

143
00:09:34.129 --> 00:09:36.115
fit the price and length preferences.

144
00:09:36.498 --> 00:09:39.784
That's a proposed rule. It isn't part of the
current app yet.

145
00:09:40.027 --> 00:09:43.859
For a simple first version, the price part of
the score could reward money

146
00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.864
left in the budget. The length part could
reward being close to the requested

147
00:09:47.911 --> 00:09:51.928
number of days. I'd start by giving those two
parts equal weight.

148
00:09:52.311 --> 00:09:56.142
But that choice needs review. Someone might
care much more about the length

149
00:09:56.188 --> 00:10:00.101
than getting the cheapest trip. For example, a
very cheap two-day trip

150
00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:02.771
may be a poor match for someone asking for a
week.

151
00:10:02.922 --> 00:10:06.451
I want the rule to reflect what the user
actually wants, not just produce

152
00:10:06.486 --> 00:10:11.002
a number. I'd also make ties predictable by
using the trip code when scores

153
00:10:11.037 --> 00:10:13.905
are equal. The result count needs a limit too.

154
00:10:14.288 --> 00:10:17.051
In the algorithm discussion, I'll call that
count k.

155
00:10:17.492 --> 00:10:21.416
If someone asks for five trips and only three
qualify, return those three.

156
00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.654
Bad budgets, bad lengths, and invalid result
counts should get a clear

157
00:10:25.735 --> 00:10:29.764
error. Both versions of the ranking code need
to follow exactly the same

158
00:10:29.799 --> 00:10:30.437
rules.

159
00:10:30.467 --> 00:10:32.406
I'd build the straightforward version first.

160
00:10:32.522 --> 00:10:36.632
Give each matching trip a score, sort the
trips from best to worst, and

161
00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:40.266
return the number requested. If the person
asks for five, take the first

162
00:10:40.301 --> 00:10:44.318
five. This version is easy to read and gives
me something to compare the

163
00:10:44.365 --> 00:10:48.776
more complicated version against. When both
use the same score and tie

164
00:10:48.823 --> 00:10:51.098
rules, they should produce the same list.

165
00:10:51.273 --> 00:10:53.119
There is a cost to sorting everything.

166
00:10:53.502 --> 00:10:57.786
If n is the number of matching trips, a
comparison sort is usually described

167
00:10:57.832 --> 00:11:03.359
as big O of n log n. That describes how the
work grows as the list gets

168
00:11:03.417 --> 00:11:05.599
larger; it isn't a stopwatch reading.

169
00:11:05.924 --> 00:11:10.127
This version also stores the scored list, so
the extra space grows with

170
00:11:10.162 --> 00:11:14.748
the number of trips. For a small catalog, that
may be completely reasonable.

171
00:11:15.027 --> 00:11:18.986
It may also be the simplest option when
someone wants most of the available

172
00:11:19.032 --> 00:11:23.049
results anyway. The code on screen is still
the current list handler.

173
00:11:23.433 --> 00:11:26.799
I'd put the new ranking function in the trip
service, rather than turn

174
00:11:26.846 --> 00:11:28.994
this controller into one long function.

175
00:11:29.597 --> 00:11:32.662
Getting trips from the database and ranking
them are different jobs.

176
00:11:32.779 --> 00:11:37.271
I'd time them separately. Otherwise, a slow
query could make the ranking

177
00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:41.428
method look slow even if most of the waiting
happened before the ranking

178
00:11:41.509 --> 00:11:42.589
code started.

179
00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.222
The other version would use a heap. A heap is
a way of keeping a small

180
00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.775
collection organized so one important item is
easy to find.

181
00:11:50.019 --> 00:11:54.512
Here, I'd keep only the best k trips seen so
far, with the worst of those

182
00:11:54.547 --> 00:11:57.624
at the top. Suppose the user wants five
results.

183
00:11:57.902 --> 00:12:03.011
Until I have five, I add each trip. After
that, a new trip only gets in

184
00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.507
if it's better than the worst trip already
kept.

185
00:12:05.948 --> 00:12:09.872
That gives me a simple rule to check as the
code runs: after each trip,

186
00:12:09.942 --> 00:12:13.192
the heap should still contain the best five
seen so far.

187
00:12:13.517 --> 00:12:16.664
If the next trip is worse than all five, I can
skip it.

188
00:12:16.989 --> 00:12:21.238
If it's better than the worst one, I swap them
and fix the heap's order.

189
00:12:21.621 --> 00:12:25.510
Equal scores still need the same trip-code tie
rule as the sorting version.

190
00:12:25.836 --> 00:12:29.319
Otherwise, the two versions could disagree
even when the scores match.

191
00:12:29.702 --> 00:12:33.324
For more than one requested result, selecting
trips this way takes big

192
00:12:33.394 --> 00:12:37.027
O of n log k. Then I sort the final k trips
for display.

193
00:12:37.236 --> 00:12:39.326
The heap only needs space for k items.

194
00:12:39.605 --> 00:12:42.914
If k is one, I can just keep the best trip
seen so far.

195
00:12:43.239 --> 00:12:47.383
One limitation is that loading every trip
first still uses memory for that

196
00:12:47.418 --> 00:12:50.321
whole list. A smaller heap doesn't remove that
cost.

197
00:12:50.495 --> 00:12:53.839
I'd need to change how trips are read to get
that larger memory saving.

198
00:12:53.867 --> 00:12:57.176
The first question is whether the ranking
returns the right trips.

199
00:12:57.501 --> 00:13:00.845
I'd test the score and tie rules on their own,
then compare the heap's

200
00:13:00.891 --> 00:13:02.772
result with the simple sorting version.

201
00:13:03.097 --> 00:13:05.175
I'd compare the full list, in order.

202
00:13:05.419 --> 00:13:09.030
Just checking that both return five items
wouldn't catch a case where one

203
00:13:09.065 --> 00:13:13.465
of those five is the wrong trip. I'd include
an empty list, one matching

204
00:13:13.523 --> 00:13:17.865
trip, fewer matches than requested, and a
request for just one result.

205
00:13:18.375 --> 00:13:20.976
I'd also try requesting all matching trips.

206
00:13:21.359 --> 00:13:24.262
Equal scores matter, so I'd include those too.

207
00:13:24.772 --> 00:13:28.476
Then I'd shuffle the input and check that the
final result stays the same.

208
00:13:28.801 --> 00:13:33.178
That's why I want a clear tie rule. I'd also
test bad numbers, invalid

209
00:13:33.224 --> 00:13:36.010
result counts, and large but valid prices.

210
00:13:36.185 --> 00:13:39.679
These cases should have agreed answers before
I start measuring speed.

211
00:13:40.062 --> 00:13:44.161
Once those checks pass, I'd try larger
generated catalogs and different

212
00:13:44.196 --> 00:13:48.039
result counts. I'd run each test more than
once, let the code warm up,

213
00:13:48.062 --> 00:13:51.301
and report the spread of the times instead of
picking the nicest number.

214
00:13:51.742 --> 00:13:55.411
I'd measure ranking by itself and the full API
request separately.

215
00:13:55.852 --> 00:13:59.277
I expect the heap to help when the user wants
a few results from a large

216
00:13:59.358 --> 00:14:04.118
list. I haven't measured that yet. If the
simpler sort is fast enough for

217
00:14:04.153 --> 00:14:06.684
this app, that's a useful result too.

218
00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:10.578
Here is the Mongoose trip model. The fields
are required, the code gets

219
00:14:10.624 --> 00:14:13.503
trimmed, and the schema asks for a unique code
index.

220
00:14:13.712 --> 00:14:15.965
There is also an index declared for the name.

221
00:14:16.209 --> 00:14:19.053
So there are already checks and indexes in the
design.

222
00:14:19.331 --> 00:14:22.907
My changes need to build on those rather than
assume the database has no

223
00:14:22.977 --> 00:14:25.914
rules at all. The gap is in what the values
mean.

224
00:14:26.123 --> 00:14:29.699
Requiring a price string doesn't prove it's a
valid price.

225
00:14:30.082 --> 00:14:33.809
In the earlier model check, a negative price
string and a made-up trip

226
00:14:33.878 --> 00:14:38.290
length passed validation. That check ran
against the model in memory; it

227
00:14:38.325 --> 00:14:40.450
didn't save those values to MongoDB.

228
00:14:40.961 --> 00:14:44.630
It still shows why I'd add number fields and
sensible ranges.

229
00:14:45.326 --> 00:14:48.589
A field being filled in is different from it
containing something the app

230
00:14:48.647 --> 00:14:53.059
can safely use. I'd also check the indexes in
the test database itself.

231
00:14:53.662 --> 00:14:57.006
Seeing unique in the schema tells me what the
code asks for.

232
00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:01.359
It doesn't confirm which indexes are present
in a running database.

233
00:15:01.603 --> 00:15:03.414
I haven't checked that live state yet.

234
00:15:03.588 --> 00:15:07.640
Before relying on the unique code rule, I'd
confirm the index exists and

235
00:15:07.698 --> 00:15:12.006
test an actual duplicate insert. For the name
index, I'd check whether

236
00:15:12.052 --> 00:15:15.953
the app's queries use it. I don't want to add
or keep indexes just because

237
00:15:15.988 --> 00:15:18.357
they sound useful without checking what they
do.

238
00:15:18.367 --> 00:15:21.281
This helper copies only the trip fields the
app allows.

239
00:15:21.455 --> 00:15:24.915
That's useful because a request could contain
extra properties that shouldn't

240
00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:29.292
be saved. The next helper checks for missing
values, null values, and empty

241
00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:32.856
strings. I'd keep those checks. But a negative
price can still be filled

242
00:15:32.914 --> 00:15:36.107
in, and a made-up length can still be a
nonempty string.

243
00:15:36.386 --> 00:15:39.555
So I also need checks that ask whether the
values make sense.

244
00:15:39.799 --> 00:15:43.723
I'd make the server, database model, and form
agree on the number fields

245
00:15:43.757 --> 00:15:47.496
and allowed ranges. Then I'd check the
existing records before changing

246
00:15:47.542 --> 00:15:51.966
them. I'd make a backup, run the conversion
without saving first, and report

247
00:15:52.012 --> 00:15:54.009
anything that couldn't be converted safely.

248
00:15:54.253 --> 00:15:57.759
After the actual update, I'd check the record
counts and converted values.

249
00:15:57.968 --> 00:16:01.579
I'd also test both adding and editing a trip,
because a rule that only

250
00:16:01.625 --> 00:16:04.806
works on new records leaves a gap when old
records are changed.

251
00:16:05.131 --> 00:16:07.291
The error helper needs a small cleanup too.

252
00:16:07.407 --> 00:16:11.227
It currently sends the database validation
message back in a details field.

253
00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:14.454
That can include internal names or rejected
values.

254
00:16:14.779 --> 00:16:18.030
I'd give the user a clear message about the
field they need to fix, while

255
00:16:18.077 --> 00:16:21.119
keeping the full technical error in controlled
server logs.

256
00:16:21.444 --> 00:16:24.973
The goal is useful feedback without sending
every database detail to the

257
00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:29.664
browser. I'd test both ordinary input mistakes
and unexpected database

258
00:16:29.698 --> 00:16:30.441
failures.

259
00:16:30.467 --> 00:16:33.044
This lookup asks for one trip by its code.

260
00:16:33.486 --> 00:16:35.900
A code index is a natural fit for that.

261
00:16:36.075 --> 00:16:39.314
The list query is different: right now it asks
for everything.

262
00:16:39.488 --> 00:16:43.737
Once I add search rules, the query might
filter by budget, dates, or trip

263
00:16:43.818 --> 00:16:48.125
length. I'd choose any new indexes based on
those actual queries.

264
00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:52.131
Adding an index to every field would create
more work without showing that

265
00:16:52.166 --> 00:16:56.914
the app is faster. To check an index, I'd use
a separate test database

266
00:16:56.972 --> 00:16:59.747
with enough sample trips to make the
comparison useful.

267
00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:04.356
I'd run the query and use MongoDB's explain
output to see how it found

268
00:17:04.402 --> 00:17:08.652
the results. I'd record how many results came
back, how many records and

269
00:17:08.721 --> 00:17:11.368
index entries it checked, and how long it
took.

270
00:17:11.577 --> 00:17:14.781
Then I'd add the proposed index and repeat the
same test.

271
00:17:15.165 --> 00:17:18.775
I'd try both a narrow search with a few
matches and a broad search with

272
00:17:18.856 --> 00:17:23.315
lots of matches. Indexes also take up space
and need updating when records

273
00:17:23.396 --> 00:17:25.614
change. That's part of the decision.

274
00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:29.851
An index may help find trips under a budget,
but it won't automatically

275
00:17:29.909 --> 00:17:33.474
speed up a preference score calculated later
in JavaScript.

276
00:17:33.717 --> 00:17:36.980
I'd keep those measurements separate so I can
tell where any improvement

277
00:17:37.026 --> 00:17:41.461
came from. This part of the project is about
learning which database changes

278
00:17:41.531 --> 00:17:45.328
help this app, and being able to show the
results instead of guessing.

279
00:17:45.334 --> 00:17:49.119
The database connection can be set through
environment variables, and there

280
00:17:49.154 --> 00:17:51.708
is code for closing it when the app shuts
down.

281
00:17:51.917 --> 00:17:55.922
Those are useful features. One thing I'd
change is this connection message.

282
00:17:56.201 --> 00:17:58.024
It prints the whole connection string.

283
00:17:58.627 --> 00:18:02.145
The local default doesn't include a password,
but a different connection

284
00:18:02.215 --> 00:18:07.335
string could. I'd print a simple connection
message or just the safe parts,

285
00:18:07.509 --> 00:18:12.444
such as the database name. I didn't find a
leaked password here; the concern

286
00:18:12.827 --> 00:18:15.857
is what this log statement could print with a
different setup.

287
00:18:16.298 --> 00:18:19.282
The seed script is another thing I'd handle
carefully.

288
00:18:19.793 --> 00:18:22.637
It deletes the trips and then loads the sample
data.

289
00:18:22.753 --> 00:18:26.190
That's fine for a throwaway development
database, but it's not something

290
00:18:26.248 --> 00:18:28.361
I'd run against records I need to keep.

291
00:18:28.535 --> 00:18:31.113
I haven't used it to wipe the working
database.

292
00:18:31.322 --> 00:18:35.199
For the new tests, I'd use a clearly separate
database and make sure the

293
00:18:35.257 --> 00:18:37.591
setup refuses to run against the wrong one.

294
00:18:37.916 --> 00:18:41.178
Cleanup should only remove data created for
those tests.

295
00:18:41.619 --> 00:18:45.160
I'd also test what happens when the database
isn't available and when the

296
00:18:45.218 --> 00:18:49.444
app closes. The README should explain how to
run these checks and which

297
00:18:49.514 --> 00:18:53.601
database they use. That makes the work easier
for someone else to repeat.

298
00:18:53.984 --> 00:18:57.676
I'd keep the original project and the record-
conversion notes too, so there

299
00:18:57.711 --> 00:19:01.588
is a clear starting point to go back to if a
change causes problems.

300
00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.990
Looking back over the review checklist, the
app already has a useful starting

301
00:19:05.037 --> 00:19:08.136
structure: separate routes, models, and two
clients.

302
00:19:08.287 --> 00:19:12.270
The main design change is to split the trip
controller into smaller jobs.

303
00:19:12.653 --> 00:19:15.660
The README and comments also give me a
starting point.

304
00:19:15.718 --> 00:19:20.095
I'd update them to explain permissions, price
units, ranking rules, and

305
00:19:20.153 --> 00:19:24.785
how to run the tests. I don't need a comment
on every line; I need explanations

306
00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.898
where someone might otherwise have to guess.

307
00:19:27.223 --> 00:19:31.345
For values and calculations, prices and trip
lengths are the big issue.

308
00:19:31.728 --> 00:19:35.931
For loops and decisions, I'd focus on making
the heap easy to check, including

309
00:19:35.989 --> 00:19:40.587
ties and empty inputs. There is also a simple
array filter in the Angular

310
00:19:40.668 --> 00:19:43.280
list that removes a deleted trip from the
screen.

311
00:19:43.663 --> 00:19:47.471
That seems reasonable for this job. I wouldn't
replace every simple loop

312
00:19:47.553 --> 00:19:49.758
just to use a more advanced data structure.

313
00:19:50.141 --> 00:19:54.425
The biggest protection gaps are admin
permissions, checks on actual values,

314
00:19:54.599 --> 00:19:57.014
and what the app reveals in errors and logs.

315
00:19:57.455 --> 00:20:01.217
JavaScript handles memory cleanup differently
from languages where I would

316
00:20:01.263 --> 00:20:06.255
free memory myself. Here, I'd watch how much
trip data gets loaded, how

317
00:20:06.337 --> 00:20:10.180
large the responses get, and whether
connections are closed properly.

318
00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:14.777
My order of work would be to settle the rules
and permissions first, keep

319
00:20:14.835 --> 00:20:19.421
the existing tests passing, and then add
ranking and database improvements.

320
00:20:19.572 --> 00:20:23.020
It's easier to judge performance once I know
the app is doing the right

321
00:20:23.067 --> 00:20:23.566
thing.

322
00:20:23.567 --> 00:20:27.073
This work also gives me a way to show the five
course outcomes.

323
00:20:27.514 --> 00:20:30.986
For working with others, I'd use instructor
feedback and write down the

324
00:20:31.032 --> 00:20:35.455
decisions that still need review. For
communication, I'd explain the changes

325
00:20:35.513 --> 00:20:39.855
in the README and project write-ups using
examples like the ones in this

326
00:20:39.948 --> 00:20:43.907
walkthrough. Someone reviewing the project
should be able to understand

327
00:20:43.977 --> 00:20:48.098
what changed and why, without having to work
it all out from the code.

328
00:20:48.481 --> 00:20:52.603
For algorithms, I'd compare sorting with the
heap, check that they return

329
00:20:52.649 --> 00:20:56.353
the same answers, and explain when each one
makes sense.

330
00:20:56.794 --> 00:21:01.612
For tools and development practices, I'd use
tests, source control, and

331
00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:06.778
the database query results. For security, I'd
work on admin permissions,

332
00:21:06.952 --> 00:21:11.643
input checks, error messages, and safe
database setup.

333
00:21:12.026 --> 00:21:14.255
Those are the things I plan to demonstrate.

334
00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:19.131
I'll report them as completed once the changes
and test results are actually

335
00:21:19.177 --> 00:21:24.669
there. The planned order is software design in
Module Three, algorithms

336
00:21:24.704 --> 00:21:29.847
and data structures in Module Four, and
databases in Module Five.

337
00:21:30.451 --> 00:21:34.932
I'll need to agree on prices and trip lengths
early because both ranking

338
00:21:35.025 --> 00:21:37.265
and the database changes depend on them.

339
00:21:37.474 --> 00:21:41.654
I'll keep the original project, make focused
changes, and use feedback

340
00:21:41.724 --> 00:21:46.739
before moving on. That covers how the app
works now, the problems I'd address,

341
00:21:46.948 --> 00:21:50.222
and how I'd check the changes. Thanks for
taking a look.
